Suggested fix part 2 (needs discussion)
The above fix will avoid the out of memory error, but the tight loop around dist.broadcast will still use all available memory before freeing all cached blocks. This is not desirable. To avoid this, I think torch.distributed.broadcast should operate solely on the caller's current stream by default, and only use a background stream if specified.

Issue

As shown in the following example, the gradient is undefined after we call tensor.view().
Steps to reproduce the behavior:
Case One: without calling  view() on input tensors.
torch::Tensor a = torch::tensor({2, 1}, torch::TensorOptions().
		requires_grad(true).dtype(torch::kFloat32));
torch::Tensor b = torch::tensor({3, 3}, torch::TensorOptions().
		requires_grad(true).dtype(torch::kFloat32));
auto c = a * b;
c.backward(); 
std::cout<<"Gradient of a"<<std::endl; 
std::cout<<a.grad()<<std::endl; // gives 3, 3 as expected
std::cout<<"Gradient of b"<<std::endl;
std::cout<<b.grad()<<std::endl; // gives 2, 1 as expected

Case Two: calling  view() on input tensors.
torch::Tensor a = torch::tensor({2, 1}, torch::TensorOptions().
		requires_grad(true).dtype(torch::kFloat32)).view({2, 1});
torch::Tensor b = torch::tensor({3, 3}, torch::TensorOptions().
		requires_grad(true).dtype(torch::kFloat32)).view({2, 1});
auto c = a * b;
c.backward(); 
std::cout<<"Gradient of a"<<std::endl; 
std::cout<<a.grad()<<std::endl; // gives undefined tensor
std::cout<<"Gradient of b"<<std::endl;
std::cout<<b.grad()<<std::endl; // gives undefined tensor

